## (1) which bytes, and why that is the first question

Frame RVAs and artifact bytes are one pair: resolving §45's frames against §46's bytes (or the reverse)
returns a different function. §46 recorded the pair explicitly — `evidence/logs/c2_mfrecords_4_4_4.log:12`
names `77c0c35e…c11ad0`, and `:172`-`:174` records that §45's set (`…86617` bytes) differs from §46's in
exactly one frame, the outermost:

    §45 in-DLL RVAs: 0x43bc, 0x55f5, 0xbd9b, 0x1ebde, 0xeede3, 0x31b9c8, 0x31cc05, 0x44d339
    §46 in-DLL RVAs: 0x43bc, 0x55f5, 0xbd9b, 0x1ebde, 0xeede3, 0x31b9c8, 0x31cc05, 0x44e399
                                                                last frame moved 0x44d339 → 0x44e399

This log reads **§46's set against §46's bytes**, because `zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll` is those
bytes right now:

    $ sha256sum zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll
    77c0c35ec5b74abc8ccb849515621aaf53abd3fdc0a5e6ab8b9466acc0c11ad0  zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll

which is the §46 artifact, not a rebuild of it.

**Correction (fix round 1).** An earlier version of this section said §45's `0x44d339` was "not
resolvable" because "no local copy of `863917f2…86617` exists on this host". That was false: the §45
digest is in the build cache, not in `/tmp` (this task's first pass searched `/tmp` only). The exact
bytes are still on disk and the maintained tool reads them:

    $ sha256sum .zig-cache/o/ecf27c97deb9b81534b0b477808ab19f/stellaris_quickjs.dll
