Frames 6–8 have no UBSan record, so they cannot be named the way frames 1–5 are. Their names come from
three checkable sources instead, and the `ip − 1` arithmetic in each is a byte-scan fact, not a guess:
the call at `0x31b9c4` is 5 bytes, so its return is `0x31b9c9` and the printed frame is `0x31b9c8`; the
call at `0x31cc01` returns `0x31cc06`, printed `0x31cc05`; the call at `0x44e395` returns `0x44e39a`,
printed `0x44e399`. Sources: (a) `list_exports` on this artifact gives `C2DiagQJSInto` = `rva 0x44e350`
(the only one of the three that is an export); (b) `quickjs_wrapper.c:497`/`:580-581` and
`exports.zig:240-241` read from disk; (c) a read-only `E8 rel32` scan of each range, which finds exactly
one call from frame 6's range to the `JS_NewRuntime2` span start (`0x3e40`), exactly one from frame 7's
range to frame 6's start (`0x31ad90`), and one from frame 8's range to frame 7's start (`0x31cbf0`).
Frame 6's range also loads the literals `"enter "`, `"heap_ok "`, `"new_rt "`, `"runtime2_null"` and
`"rt=%p "` at `0x31af50`, `0x31b291`, `0x31b926`, `0x31badd` and `0x31bc85` — the DLOG sequence that
only `c2_diag_sequence` emits in that source file. These are attributions of the *range to a source
definition*, not link-time symbol names; the artifact still has no symbol table.

The offsets are what a *return address* must look like, so they are recorded per frame rather than folded
into the span. All eight are inside their own span, as they must be.

So the in-DLL chain, innermost first, is:

    js_rc  (quickjs.c:1494, the UBSan trap)
      ^ called from js_alloc_string_rt  (:2355; frame 2 sits +211 into its 737-byte body)
      ^ called from __JS_NewAtomInit    (:3426; frame 3 sits +46 into its 500-byte body)
      ^ called from JS_InitAtoms        (:3169; frame 4 sits +725 into its 1003-byte body)
      ^ called from JS_NewRuntime2      (:2067; frame 5 sits +1404 into its 4395-byte body)
      ^ called from c2_diag_sequence    (quickjs_wrapper.c:497; frame 6 sits +3128 into its 7771-byte body)
      ^ called from C2DiagQuickJS       (quickjs_wrapper.c:580; frame 7 sits +21 into its 320-byte body)
      ^ called from C2DiagQJSInto       (src/dll/exports.zig:240; frame 8 sits +73 into its 124-byte body)

**Every edge of that chain is independently present in the source**, which is what makes it a call chain
and not eight coincident ranges:

    quickjs.c:2096   if (JS_InitAtoms(rt))                          — inside JS_NewRuntime2   (5 → 4)
    quickjs.c:3191   if (__JS_NewAtomInit(rt, p, len, atom_type) …  — inside JS_InitAtoms      (4 → 3)
    quickjs.c:3430   p = js_alloc_string_rt(rt, len, 0);            — inside __JS_NewAtomInit  (3 → 2)
    quickjs.c:2361   js_rc(str)->ref_count = 1;                     — inside js_alloc_string_rt(2 → 1)

and frame 2's record list contains `:2361:17` on type `JSMallocBlockHeader` — the same header type as the
trap — so the caller of the trapping frame is carrying a check on that very call.

Three things this pins that §45 could only infer (`c2_selfabort_4_4_4.log:163-166`: "The frame between the
trap and `__JS_NewAtomInit` (RVA 0xeede3 …) has no `.pdata` entry covering it … the middle of the chain is
inferred"):

  1. **0xeede3 has a `.pdata` entry, and §45's own bytes say so too.** On the cached §45 artifact the
     same `func` gives `rva 0xeed10..0xeeff1` (737 B) with the same six records (`:2361`-`:2366`);
     verbatim output in (4a). So §45's sentence "has no `.pdata` entry covering it"
     (`c2_selfabort_4_4_4.log:163-166`) is not a property of those bytes and not a difference between
     the two builds — it is contradicted by its own artifact. This is also **not** explained by a tool
     version: the frozen original and the maintained tool share the same `.pdata` walk
     (`scripts/scratch/2026-10_abortexit/ubsan_site.py:119-131` and `scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py:213-225`
     are the same `size // 12` loop with the same `0 < begin < end` filter), and running the frozen
     original on the §45 bytes reproduces the span and the six records. No `func 0xeede3` invocation is
     recorded in §45 — the claim appears only as prose — so there is no old bound computation to blame.
     The only parsing bound that changed after §45 is `iter_records`' scan end (`end - 32` →
     `end - RECORD_READ_SIZE + 1`, `scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py:192`, commit 26589ac), which governs
     which *records* a `site` scan can see, not which function spans exist. On the bytes read here —
     and on §45's — the middle of the chain is attributed, not inferred.
  2. `__JS_NewAtomInit`'s span on §46 bytes is `0x1ebb0..0x1eda4` — numerically identical to §45's
     (`c2_selfabort_4_4_4.log:101`), so the recorder that moved the outer frame did not move this one.
  3. The 131-byte trapping function carries **exactly one** UBSan record (`:1494:12`). No second site in
     that range competes for the attribution, so the "which line of this function" question has one
     answer in these bytes. (Later tasks that change the build should re-measure this, not assume it.)

